    "provisionalDifficulty": 5,
    "stem": "You are a physician dispatched to assist on-scene incident commanders at a mass-casualty incident following the collapse of a pedestrian walkway in a crowded shopping concourse. More than 40 victims are scattered across the plaza with visible injuries. Emergency medical services personnel are employing the Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment (START) algorithm. You are asked to triage an adult victim trapped under light debris. The victim is a 32-year-old woman. After clearing the light debris from her torso, you observe that she is breathing spontaneously at a respiratory rate of 34 breaths/min. Her radial pulse is rapid and weak, and her capillary refill time is 3.5 seconds. She is confused, groaning incoherently, and unable to follow simple commands such as 'squeeze my fingers.' According to the START triage protocol, which of the following triage tags should be immediately affixed to this patient?",
    "choices": [
      [
        "Red Tag (Immediate)",
        "The SIMPLE TRIAGE AND RAPID TREATMENT (START) algorithm is the most widely utilized prehospital mass-casualty triage methodology in the United States. Its goal is to prioritize the delivery of limited medical and transport resources to maximize overall survival. START evaluates adult casualties in less than 60 seconds through a sequential hierarchy: (1) WALKING WOUNDED: anyone who can walk to a designated area is tagged GREEN (Minor). (2) RESPIRATIONS: if not breathing, open airway; if breathing resumes, tag RED (Immediate); if apnea persists, tag BLACK (Deceased/Expectant). If spontaneous breathing is > 30 BREATHS/MIN, tag RED IMMEDIATELY without checking further. If respiratory rate is < 30/min, proceed to: (3) PERFUSION: check radial pulse or capillary refill. If radial pulse is ABSENT or capillary refill > 2 SECONDS, tag RED (Immediate) and control life-threatening hemorrhage. If perfusion is normal, proceed to: (4) MENTAL STATUS: can the patient follow simple commands? If unable to obey commands, tag RED (Immediate). If able to obey commands, tag YELLOW (Delayed). In this case, the patient fails all three physiological gates: tachypneic at 34/min (> 30), delayed capillary refill at 3.5s (> 2s), and unable to follow commands. She unambiguously meets criteria for RED (IMMEDIATE) TRIAGE, requiring emergent stabilization and priority transport."
      ],
      [
        "Yellow Tag (Delayed)",
        "Yellow is assigned to victims who have serious injuries requiring intervention but possess normal respirations (<= 30), normal perfusion (radial pulse present, refill <= 2s), and normal mental status (able to obey commands)."
      ],
      [
        "Green Tag (Minor)",
        "Green is reserved for the 'walking wounded'\u2014conscious casualties with minor injuries who can understand instructions and ambulatory evacuate the scene independently."
      ],
      [
        "Black Tag (Deceased / Expectant)",
        "Black is assigned only to non-breathing victims whose apnea persists despite simple airway repositioning, or catastrophic injuries incompatible with life under disaster conditions."
      ],
      [
        "Blue Tag (Palliative Only)",
        "Blue tags are not a standard category in the four-color START disaster triage algorithm (which consists strictly of Red, Yellow, Green, and Black)."
      ]
    ],
    "shortestValidSolution": "START triage: Respirations > 30/min (or cap refill > 2s, or cannot follow commands) = Red Tag (Immediate).",
    "necessaryInference": "Apply the START disaster triage RPM algorithm: Respirations > 30, Perfusion absent/delayed, or Mental status impaired assigns Red (Immediate) tag.",
    "strongestDistractor": "Yellow Tag (Delayed)",
    "distractorExclusion": "Yellow is only for patients who pass all three physiological gates (respirations <= 30, capillary refill <= 2s, follows commands); this patient fails all three.",
    "citation": "Kahn CA, Schultz CH, Miller KT, Anderson CL. Does START triage work? An outcomes assessment after a disaster. Ann Emerg Med. 2009;54(3):424-430. PMID: 19345434.",
