        "MEDICAL AID IN DYING (MAID; also termed physician-assisted dying or Death with Dignity) is legally authorized in several US jurisdictions (e.g., Oregon, Washington, California, Colorado, Vermont, Hawaii, New Jersey, Maine, New Mexico, and Washington DC). In all jurisdictions with MAID legislation, RIGID STATUTORY SAFEGUARDS are legally codified to ensure voluntariness and protect vulnerable patients: (1) ELIGIBILITY: The patient must be an adult (>= 18 years), a state resident, possess INTACT DECISION-MAKING CAPACITY, and have a TERMINAL PROGNOSIS OF <= 6 MONTHS; (2) TWO PHYSICIAN CONFIRMATIONS: Both the prescribing attending physician and an independent consulting physician must verify diagnosis, prognosis, mental capacity, and freedom from coercion; (3) MANDATORY REQUESTS: The patient must make TWO VOLUNTARY ORAL REQUESTS separated by a statutory waiting period (historically 15 days; some states waive this if death is imminent within 48 hours), plus ONE WRITTEN REQUEST signed in the presence of two independent witnesses (neither of whom can be an heir or treating clinician); (4) PSYCHIATRIC CONSULTATION: Required if there is any question of depression or impaired judgment; and (5) CRITICAL BOUNDARY: THE PATIENT MUST PERSONALLY SELF-ADMINISTER THE MEDICATION (oral ingestion). EUTHANASIA (administration by a physician, nurse, or family member) IS STRICTLY ILLEGAL and constitutes homicide under US law."
