        "The INTENTION-TO-TREAT (ITT) PRINCIPLE is the gold standard method for analyzing randomized controlled trials (RCTs). Under ITT, ALL PARTICIPANTS ARE ANALYZED IN THE GROUP TO WHICH THEY WERE ORIGINALLY RANDOMIZED, regardless of protocol deviations, non-compliance, dropouts, or crossover ('once randomized, always analyzed'). The primary methodological rationales for ITT are: (1) PRESERVING RANDOMIZATION: Randomization ensures that both known and unknown baseline prognostic factors and confounders are equally distributed between treatment arms. Analyzing participants by actual treatment received ('as-treated' or 'per-protocol') destroys this balance because patients who cross over (e.g., sicker patients whose angina worsens) are systematically different from those who adhere, introducing severe CONFOUNDING BY INDICATION AND ATTRITION BIAS; (2) REFLECTING REAL-WORLD EFFECTIVENESS: ITT mimics real-world clinical practice where patients frequently miss doses, discontinue drugs due to side effects, or require rescue interventions; and (3) AVOIDING INFLATION OF TYPE I ERROR: ITT yields a conservative estimate of treatment effect. Per-protocol analysis is reserved as a secondary sensitivity analysis to evaluate maximal biological efficacy in fully adherent subjects."
